Gas Tracker

Android app for tracking fuel usage and cost for one car, assuming a full-tank refill each time. Includes a mirrored Python reference implementation of the core logic.

Milestones

1. Manual entry and dashboard
   - Enter distance driven (or use odometer), fuel volume, total cost, date, octane, station.
   - Units: km/mi for distance, liters/gallons for volume; home/input currencies for cost.
   - Remembers last selections.
   - Dashboard with windowed metrics: cost per period, efficiency (L/100km, MPG), charts.
   - Handles missing values by interpolation, merges same-day refills, converts currencies via ECB/frankfurter.dev rates cached locally.
   - Edit/delete refills, settings for home currency and defaults, 2-year sample data.

2. Odometer-based input
   - Enter the current odometer reading instead of distance.
   - Distance is computed automatically from consecutive readings.
   - Same-day merges work with odometer readings.

3. Photo / OCR entry
   - Tesseract OCR runs on-device; standard `eng.traineddata` is bundled gzip-compressed
     and decompressed into `files/tessdata/` on first use.
   - User can take a photo with the camera or pick from the gallery.
   - Photos are auto-classified as pump, odometer, or receipt using OCR-text heuristics.
   - Extracted numbers are shown as cached drafts at the top of the Log form and prefilled into the fields.
   - Drafts (image + parsed values) are persisted in `files/photo_cache.json` and `files/photos/` so they survive app restarts.
   - Drafts are cleared when the refill is saved or editing is cancelled.
   - Receipts are detected but extra fields (station, items) are not yet extracted.

4. Reliability (v0.3.0)
   - Versioned refill JSON, backup file, fail-closed loads, export/import.
   - FX failures stay unavailable; consumption uses paired distance/volume; sparse windows do not extrapolate.
   - Privacy and backup rules, accessibility strings, and CI.
   - APK size: gzipped OCR model, compressed JNI, drop 32-bit x86, release R8.

Notes

- Camera capture is implemented via a FileProvider; it works on real devices. The emulator camera app on the test AVD crashes, so camera was verified via intent setup and gallery flow only on the emulator.
- OCR classification is rule-based and can be improved with more samples.

Building and testing

Python (repo root):
  python3 -m pip install -e ".[test]"
  python3 -m pytest -q

Android (from android/, JDK 21):
  ./gradlew :app:testDebugUnitTest :app:assembleDebug

Instrumented test (emulator must be running; not part of CI):
  ./gradlew :app:connectedDebugAndroidTest

Debug APK output:
  android/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk
